Bottomline by Groww
19500 subscribers
68 likes
2282 views
6 Jun 2025
India’s biggest carmaker—known for reliability, reach, and scale—is no longer cruising. Its market share has dropped to a 12-year low of 41%, down from 50% just a few years ago. And despite record profits in FY25, the cracks are showing.

In this episode of Bottomline by Groww, we unpack how rising input costs, slowing entry-level demand, a delayed EV play, and aggressive competition from Tata, Hyundai-Kia, and Mahindra are reshaping Maruti’s future. We also break down the company’s pivot—new launches, export bets, hybrid strategy, and ₹9,000 crore in capex—but will it be enough?

Key takeaways:
✅ FY25 profits up 7.5%, but EBIT down in Q4
✅ Market share at 12-year low; SUV & CNG mix declined
✅ EV entry delayed to FY26; hybrid share at just 2.4%
✅ ₹96,000 crore reserves fund aggressive capex and exports
✅ Export volumes at record 3.3 lakh; aiming for 7.5–8 lakh by FY31
✅ Flexible manufacturing to produce petrol, hybrid, and EVs together
✅ Suzuki’s stake in Maruti is now worth more than Suzuki itself

This is more than just an earnings breakdown—it’s a turning point for India’s automotive icon.
Is Maruti still the gold standard - or just trying to keep up?
Tell us what you think in the comments, and subscribe to Bottomline by Groww for more deep dives like this.

