- Maruti Suzuki Competitive Position Analysis | PDF | Car | Business: # Maruti Suzuki Competitive Position Analysis. ## Uploaded by. AI-enhanced title and description. This executive summary analyzes Maruti Suzuki's competitive position in the Indian passenger car market. For many years, Maruti Suzuki has dominated the market, holding around 51% market share, while its nearest competitor Hyundai has only 16%. A Porter's Five Forces analysis found the industry faces high rivalry among competing brands. Maruti Suzuki has strengths in its established distribution network, Japanese technology and expertise, and brand positioning for affordability and legacy. However, it faces challenges from new regulations requiring investment and innovations in electric vehicles threaten its competitive advantages. Overall, the analysis finds Maruti Suzuki well positioned due to its capabilities, but it must adapt to changes in the industry environment and technology. ### Read this document in other languages. ## Share this document. ## Footer menu. ## Support. ## Get our free apps. Scribd - Download on the App Store. Scribd - Get it on Google Play.
- Is Maruti Suzuki losing its grip on the auto market? - YouTube: Is Maruti Suzuki losing its grip on the auto market?
Bottomline by Groww
19500 subscribers
68 likes
2282 views
6 Jun 2025
India’s biggest carmaker—known for reliability, reach, and scale—is no longer cruising. Its market share has dropped to a 12-year low of 41%, down from 50% just a few years ago. And despite record profits in FY25, the cracks are showing.

In this episode of Bottomline by Groww, we unpack how rising input costs, slowing entry-level demand, a delayed EV play, and aggressive competition from Tata, Hyundai-Kia, and Mahindra are reshaping Maruti’s future. We also break down the company’s pivot—new launches, export bets, hybrid strategy, and ₹9,000 crore in capex—but will it be enough?

Key takeaways:
✅ FY25 profits up 7.5%, but EBIT down in Q4
✅ Market share at 12-year low; SUV & CNG mix declined
✅ EV entry delayed to FY26; hybrid share at just 2.4%
✅ ₹96,000 crore reserves fund aggressive capex and exports
✅ Export volumes at record 3.3 lakh; aiming for 7.5–8 lakh by FY31
✅ Flexible manufacturing to produce petrol, hybrid, and EVs together
✅ Suzuki’s stake in Maruti is now worth more than Suzuki itself

This is more than just an earnings breakdown—it’s a turning point for India’s automotive icon.
Is Maruti still the gold standard - or just trying to keep up?
Tell us what you think in the comments, and subscribe to Bottomline by Groww for more deep dives like this.

Time Stamps:
00:00 - Disclaimer  
